/*
:name: bitrange_dyn_narrow
:type: equiv
:verilog_top: bitrange_dyn_narrow.rsel_n
:pyrope_top: rsel_n
*/

// Runtime contiguous bit-range select (`a#[lo ..= hi]`, the same NATIVE form
// bitrange_dyn covers) but with a NARROW low endpoint, which is what makes this
// pair distinct coverage rather than a duplicate.
//
// tolg's lower_dynamic_range_select emits `(a>>lo) & ((1<<(hi-lo+1))-1)`, so the
// shift AMOUNT here is `1 + base + len - base`. Verilog SELF-determines a
// shift's amount operand -- it is the one binary operand that does NOT inherit
// the expression's context width -- so an amount cgen folds inline is evaluated
// at its own natural width. With `base:u1` that natural width is only
// max(2,1,3,1) = 3 bits while the real range is 1..8 (LiveHD infers 6), so
// `len==7` wrapped 8 to 0: the `(1<<n)-1` mask came out 0 and `z` read 0 for
// every base (74 of 640 vectors wrong, iverilog-measured) until cgen started
// landing a shift amount in its own DECLARED variable (the shift-amount pass at
// the end of Cgen_verilog::create_locals) instead of folding it into the shift
// text -- a declaration carries the inferred width AND the signedness, which an
// in-place `({N{1'b0}} | ...)` pad cannot.
//
// bitrange_dyn does NOT catch this: with `base:u4` the same amount happens to be
// 4 bits wide, which already holds 8. The `#[base ..= (base+len)]` shape keeps
// `hi >= lo` for every input, so there is no descending-range corner case.
pub comb rsel_n(a:u16, base:u1, len:u3) -> (z:u16) {
  z = a#[base ..= (base+len)]
}
